# conversations-emotions.kb - Conversations / Emotions
# Format: concept | intent | answer | keywords | patterns | tl | bis
# 6 entries, one genuinely distinct fact per line.

jealousy | definition | Jealousy is the fear of losing something or somebody to another, and the word carries a strong assumption that the threat is real, which is what separates it from envy. Envy is wanting what somebody else has, which may be perfectly attainable; jealousy is the fear of being displaced. In conversation the distinction decides whether a complaint sounds like a request for something or an accusation, and treating them as the same is what makes jealous arguments hard to win. | jealousy, envy, fear, threat, displace, accusation | what is jealousy, difference between jealousy and envy, why jealousy is different | jealousy | pagkabunyi
pride | definition | Pride is a positive feeling about something, and English splits it into being proud of, which is ordinary, and pride in a work or a possession, which carries a faint defensiveness. That defensive sense is why proud of can sound self-satisfied to a listener who is not, and the word travels badly across cultures. National and family pride are the two commonest everyday uses, and both can carry an assumption about who does not share them. | pride, proud, defens, self, national, family | what is pride, difference between proud and prideful, is proud negative | pride | kabalatan
relief | definition | Relief is the easing of a worry once the danger has passed, and it is a distinct emotion because it depends on having first been afraid. That dependency is what makes it odd when reported alone, since relief without a prior worry is hard to imagine. In conversation it is one of the few feelings people describe with a physical sensation, most often the dropping of tension in the shoulders or the breath coming back. | relief, worry, danger, ease, fear, breath | what is relief, why does relief feel physical, is relief an emotion | relief | kaginhawa
disappointment | definition | Disappointment is the gap between what was expected and what happened, and the word covers anything from a small letdown to a serious loss, which is why it cannot be read as serious by itself. It is also the feeling least like to be heard, because the person complaining is usually treated as low-maintenance, and the listener responds by raising the expectations next time. A useful sentence is to name the gap explicitly rather than saying the outcome was bad. | disappointment, expectation, gap, letdown, complaint, raise | what is disappointment, difference between disappointment and sadness, how to complain about a result | disappointment | pagkabigo
loneliness | definition | Loneliness is a feeling of being separated, and English keeps it carefully apart from solitude, which is a chosen condition, and from being alone, which is only a fact. Solitude can be restful, loneliness rarely is, and the difference is the whole point. The other thing that gets confused is isolation, which is being separated by circumstance rather than by feeling, and the two can describe the same situation for different people. | loneliness, solitude, alone, isolated, chosen, feeling | what is loneliness, difference between loneliness and solitude, is loneliness the same as being alone | loneliness | kalungkutan
contentment | definition | Contentment is a settled, unexcited satisfaction, and English marks it as a distinct emotional state rather than a mild form of happiness. Happiness wants more; contentment has stopped wanting. That is why contentment appears in retirement and in simple circumstances, and why it is slightly suspicious in advertising, since wanting somebody to be content is a much easier sell than wanting them to be happy. The everyday form is content, and the noun is rarer than the adjective. | contentment, satisfaction, settle, happiness, want, simple | what is contentment, difference between contentment and happiness, is contentment an emotion | contentment | katiyakan
